WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.810
In this episode of the podcast, I have an
amazing guest, who has come a long way from

00:00:05.810 --> 00:00:11.750
reporting unexploitable bugs to Google, which
were denied, through valid bugs and being

00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:18.730
on their hall of fame, up until working at
Google and improving browser security mechanisms.

00:00:18.730 --> 00:00:21.810
The name of my guest is Michał Bendkowski.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:22.810
Enjoy.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:24.130
Hello, Michał.

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:28.660
Thank you so much for joining me in today's
podcast.

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:34.130
To start off, can you tell me and our audience,
what is your background and how did you get

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:36.270
to the point you are currently at?

00:00:36.270 --> 00:00:38.810
Start wherever you want to start.

00:00:38.810 --> 00:00:41.190
Hey, everyone.

00:00:41.190 --> 00:00:43.570
Hey, Greg.

00:00:43.570 --> 00:00:54.820
So, I started my career in cybersecurity around
10 years ago.

00:00:54.820 --> 00:01:02.850
I was a contestant for many, many years, and
that was like my main job.

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:14.720
I was always interested more in attacking
than prevention.

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:24.820
In 2014, I think, or 2013, I started Bug Bounty
as a kind of a side job.

00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:32.220
I was hacking mainly Google programs, like
Google Web VRP and the Chromium.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:39.810
Well, I also imported some bugs in some other
Bug Bounty platforms.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:51.229
After many, many years working as an attacker,
at some point, I decided that instead of finding

00:01:51.229 --> 00:01:59.100
an XSS number 100, maybe it will make more
sense in trying to do some stuff that will

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:02.810
prevent this XSS from happening in the future.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:10.310
Or maybe if we cannot prevent that, then maybe
we can do something that will mitigate the

00:02:10.310 --> 00:02:12.920
side effects of this vulnerability.

00:02:12.920 --> 00:02:23.550
So, in essence, I was on the offensive side
both in my daily job and in bug hunting.

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:31.200
But recently, I started my shift from the
offensive to the defense.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:34.420
And where do you work now?

00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.890
So, now I work at Google.

00:02:36.890 --> 00:02:40.160
I joined Google at the beginning of this year.

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:45.450
I am in the information security engineering
team.

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:52.250
We actually recently started a blog on bughunters.google.com,
which is like the blog specifically of our

00:02:52.250 --> 00:02:55.390
team, which I think is pretty nice.

00:02:55.390 --> 00:02:59.430
And this team does many different things.

00:02:59.430 --> 00:03:09.670
But in essence, what I'm interested in is
trying to prevent as many bug classes as possible.

00:03:09.670 --> 00:03:14.170
And the perfect way to prevent a bug class
would be if that would be possible in the

00:03:14.170 --> 00:03:15.170
web platform.

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:22.140
So, a developer of an application doesn't
have to do anything, but we make some changes

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:28.479
in the specifications or in the implementations
of browsers.

00:03:28.479 --> 00:03:32.640
And thanks to that, everybody is safer, basically.

00:03:32.640 --> 00:03:39.610
Yeah, that's very annoying to us attackers
when browsers make changes, because then it

00:03:39.610 --> 00:03:43.750
applies to pretty much every single application
at once.

00:03:43.750 --> 00:03:48.920
Whereas, when it's some changes in the server-side
frameworks, like, I don't know, introducing

00:03:48.920 --> 00:03:58.060
prepared statements for SQL, it takes years,
long years before everyone uses the new change.

00:03:58.060 --> 00:04:05.130
But in browsers, it's pretty much instantly
after it gets to the stable branch of Chromium

00:04:05.130 --> 00:04:07.720
or whatever, it's just everywhere.

00:04:07.720 --> 00:04:10.500
So, that's something I don't like.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:14.090
Yeah, I think from my perspective, that's
pretty cool.

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:21.430
Even if the application is not supported at
all, but people update their browsers and

00:04:21.430 --> 00:04:28.789
the auto-updating mechanisms in browsers are
pretty good, then they can still profit from

00:04:28.789 --> 00:04:30.370
the changes that we make.

00:04:30.370 --> 00:04:37.730
And yeah, in a nutshell, my job now is that
the more I will annoy you, the better.

00:04:37.730 --> 00:04:40.760
Yeah, unfortunately, that's true.

00:04:40.760 --> 00:04:48.300
What back class are you working on at the
moment?

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:52.690
I'm trying to prevent mutation XSS.

00:04:52.690 --> 00:04:57.280
That's one of it.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:04.030
Basically, there is a change that I made in
Chromium a few months ago to change how HTML

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:10.169
is serialized, maybe how DOM tree is serialized
to the HTML.

00:05:10.169 --> 00:05:17.950
Because the mutation XSS, like the root cause
of mutation XSS, is that if you have a DOM

00:05:17.950 --> 00:05:23.840
tree and then you serialize it and then you
parse it again, then you are not guaranteed

00:05:23.840 --> 00:05:29.220
that the first DOM tree will be equivalent
to the second DOM tree.

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:35.290
I think, by the way, that it is a really bad
feature of HTML, because imagine that if you

00:05:35.290 --> 00:05:41.889
had a JSON, if you had an object, and you
would serialize an object to JSON, and then

00:05:41.889 --> 00:05:47.100
you would deserialize this JSON to an object,
and you would get a completely different object,

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:49.460
then you would say that it is pretty bad.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:54.250
But this is exactly what happens in the HTML,
and it is actually by design.

00:05:54.250 --> 00:06:02.240
There is an actual warning in the HTML specification
that when you do a run strip of HTML and a

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:09.190
DOM tree, you are not guaranteed to get the
same stuff that you got before.

00:06:09.190 --> 00:06:17.340
There are basically two main surfaces on which
we need to work with.

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:26.130
The first one is deserialization of attributes,
because there was a decision made in the HTML

00:06:26.130 --> 00:06:34.949
working group, and that was like 10, 15 years
ago, I think, that characters such as the

00:06:34.949 --> 00:06:41.400
less than character and the greater than character
are not escaped in attributes.

00:06:41.400 --> 00:06:48.310
This is actually bad, because it might happen
that something that was supposed to be an

00:06:48.310 --> 00:06:53.340
attribute will be mistreated as an open stack.

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:58.340
It is actually a really slight change that
they are trying to make, is that when you

00:06:58.340 --> 00:07:04.979
serialize an attribute, then the less than
character is serialized to ampersand lt and

00:07:04.979 --> 00:07:07.640
the greater than to ampersand gt.

00:07:07.640 --> 00:07:13.280
But what's also interesting and something
that I need to work with right now is that

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.740
we added a use counter to Chromium.

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:23.240
So use counter is basically when we try to
make a change that we believe can be backwards

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:29.050
incompatible, then we are adding counters
to Chromium, and we want to see how many people

00:07:29.050 --> 00:07:34.810
actually use a feature, so how likely it is
to break the web.

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:44.580
And it turns out that, if I remember correctly,
around 5% of page loads serialize attributes

00:07:44.580 --> 00:07:49.379
with less than or greater than characters
in them, which is much greater than I had

00:07:49.379 --> 00:07:51.379
expected before.

00:07:51.379 --> 00:07:58.110
It doesn't mean that if we make the change,
like to say with Chromium, the sites will

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:00.940
break, but there is a potential that they
might break.

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:07.050
So we are doing some kind of analysis to see
if this will really happen.

00:08:07.050 --> 00:08:12.259
And another problem, besides attributes, is
that some tags, such as style, this is the

00:08:12.259 --> 00:08:19.470
most prominent example, when you serialize
the style, then you don't do any escaping.

00:08:19.470 --> 00:08:24.360
So inside style, the less than character is
just serialized as the less than character

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:26.750
without the entity.

00:08:26.750 --> 00:08:29.800
And this also can be abused in mutation access.

00:08:29.800 --> 00:08:38.140
There is basically no way in the HTML spec
to safely serialize a style tag, because if

00:08:38.140 --> 00:08:45.769
it contains the nstack for, I mean, if you
use the DOM API and you set like style.textContent

00:08:45.769 --> 00:08:51.640
is equal to nstack to style and then some
stuff after that, then there is no way to

00:08:51.640 --> 00:08:53.450
serialize that safely.

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:56.950
And yeah, I'm also trying to do something
about that.

00:08:56.950 --> 00:08:59.610
But this is actually super, super tricky.

00:08:59.610 --> 00:09:05.430
And I am not even sure if that's possible
at this moment.

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:09.050
You know, I mean, it is possible, but can
we do that without breaking the web?

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:11.709
That's the question.

00:09:11.709 --> 00:09:16.079
But when it comes to the attributes, it's
also tricky because of the highest percentage

00:09:16.079 --> 00:09:17.079
of usage.

00:09:17.079 --> 00:09:24.330
So yeah, we need to do some more analysis
before we can say that it is safe to flip

00:09:24.330 --> 00:09:27.839
this flag in the stable chromium.

00:09:27.839 --> 00:09:36.060
By the way, if you use the chromium flag called
enable experimental web platform features,

00:09:36.060 --> 00:09:41.230
then the serialization algorithm is changed
to the attribute.

00:09:41.230 --> 00:09:47.310
So if you use that flag and if you see that
anything breaks, please report it back to

00:09:47.310 --> 00:09:51.320
chromium or let me know, because I would be
very interested in any use case that it actually

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:52.330
breaks.

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:56.750
I use that flag on a daily basis for a few
months now, and I don't think I saw anything

00:09:56.750 --> 00:09:59.730
breaking, but that's just like anecdotal.

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:01.010
One person.

00:10:01.010 --> 00:10:02.920
That's not enough.

00:10:02.920 --> 00:10:08.650
I think it can also, if I understand this
correctly, it can also break some CSP bypasses

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:15.149
where you could only inject an attribute and
then you used, I think, bootstrap with data

00:10:15.149 --> 00:10:22.680
HTML attribute and the value of the attribute
became an actual HTML.

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:27.230
So if I understand correctly, this will also
break the CSP bypass.

00:10:27.230 --> 00:10:34.490
I mean, if you use a DOM API like get attribute
or like a data set and you get the value of

00:10:34.490 --> 00:10:35.970
an attribute, that it doesn't change.

00:10:35.970 --> 00:10:37.250
Like the parsing doesn't change.

00:10:37.250 --> 00:10:38.250
Okay.

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:39.250
Only the serialization.

00:10:39.250 --> 00:10:42.190
So I don't think that this bypasses will.

00:10:42.190 --> 00:10:43.190
Okay.

00:10:43.190 --> 00:10:47.950
So serialization here means using what exactly?

00:10:47.950 --> 00:10:53.040
That you have a DOM tree and you create a
textual output from the DOM tree.

00:10:53.040 --> 00:10:54.040
Okay.

00:10:54.040 --> 00:10:57.519
So you create an HTML text from the DOM tree.

00:10:57.519 --> 00:11:03.329
Because maybe let me explain why is this a
problem, because I think I didn't explain

00:11:03.329 --> 00:11:04.329
that.

00:11:04.329 --> 00:11:13.220
The problem is like if you have an HTML sanitizer
and well, HTML sanitizers usually work this

00:11:13.220 --> 00:11:18.790
way, that you have an input string that you
consider able and it outputs another string

00:11:18.790 --> 00:11:21.779
that is supposed to be safe.

00:11:21.779 --> 00:11:31.779
But what happens internally is that the HTML
sanitizer parses the HTML, it traverses the

00:11:31.779 --> 00:11:37.269
DOM tree and it removes everything that it
considers bad.

00:11:37.269 --> 00:11:41.959
And then it needs to serialize to a textual
output to a string.

00:11:41.959 --> 00:11:48.990
But then when you do something like if that
inner HTML is equal to like DOM purify sanitize

00:11:48.990 --> 00:11:53.040
something, then the HTML is parsed again.

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:54.040
Okay.

00:11:54.040 --> 00:11:59.029
Because the sanitizer serialized the DOM tree.

00:11:59.029 --> 00:12:03.829
But when you assign something to inner HTML,
then it is parsed again.

00:12:03.829 --> 00:12:09.959
And that's the problem because you might set
a DOM tree that the sanitizer considers safe,

00:12:09.959 --> 00:12:13.260
but it is not safe after another reparsing.

00:12:13.260 --> 00:12:16.410
That's basically a mutation exercise.

00:12:16.410 --> 00:12:26.449
So the problem is that, for instance, one
example of a mutation exercise that actually

00:12:26.449 --> 00:12:28.350
happened in Google a few years ago.

00:12:28.350 --> 00:12:32.150
There's a video by Live Overflow about it.

00:12:32.150 --> 00:12:34.449
It was done by Masato Kinagawa.

00:12:34.449 --> 00:12:39.950
Basically, you have a no script element in
the HTML specification.

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:46.970
And it is a very interesting element because
it is parsed differently depending on whether

00:12:46.970 --> 00:12:50.160
you have scripting enabled or disabled.

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:54.790
So if your scripting is enabled, then no script
is treated pretty much as style.

00:12:54.790 --> 00:12:57.050
I mean, it is parsed pretty much the same
way.

00:12:57.050 --> 00:12:59.949
So everything inside is treated as text.

00:12:59.949 --> 00:13:05.430
But if you have scripting disabled, then it
is parsed as this, basically.

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:08.050
So two completely different parsing modes.

00:13:08.050 --> 00:13:19.090
And the problem is that if you use the browser
provided HTML parser, such as DOM parser,

00:13:19.090 --> 00:13:24.550
parse from string, then it parses the HTML
as if the scripting was disabled.

00:13:24.550 --> 00:13:28.040
By default, is it disabled or enabled?

00:13:28.040 --> 00:13:29.089
Disabled.

00:13:29.089 --> 00:13:30.089
And you can change that.

00:13:30.089 --> 00:13:34.510
It is assumed that the scripting flag is disabled,
and it is always parsed this way.

00:13:34.510 --> 00:13:37.449
There is no way in the API to change that.

00:13:37.449 --> 00:13:41.540
But when you assign to innerHTML, then you
obviously assign it to a document which has

00:13:41.540 --> 00:13:42.910
the scripting enabled.

00:13:42.910 --> 00:13:51.670
So the problem is that the sanitizer parses
the no script kind of differently than how

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:53.470
it will be parsed afterwards.

00:13:53.470 --> 00:14:01.100
So suddenly, you might have a DOM tree that
the sanitizer considers safe.

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:06.660
And after that reparsing, and the difference
in no script, it is no longer safe.

00:14:06.660 --> 00:14:12.509
There are other examples where after a few
parsing trips, you can have very different

00:14:12.509 --> 00:14:13.740
effects.

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:19.180
But I think this is the easiest one to explain
why this is a problem, why this might occur.

00:14:19.180 --> 00:14:24.589
And by the way, if you use a sanitizer that
exposes, like client-side sanitizer, that

00:14:24.589 --> 00:14:32.550
exposes an API that it can return not a string,
but a DOM node, then use it, because then

00:14:32.550 --> 00:14:37.040
you don't have any problem with mutation access,
because the HTML is not reparsed.

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:38.350
Yeah, yeah.

00:14:38.350 --> 00:14:45.500
These things, I got a little bit into like
knowing the more advanced or hidden, I don't

00:14:45.500 --> 00:14:51.269
know, you can say features of HTML, like namespaces
and types of nodes and everything you say

00:14:51.269 --> 00:14:53.360
like here.

00:14:53.360 --> 00:14:57.850
So guys, if you are new to this, I recommend
you blog posts by Michal.

00:14:57.850 --> 00:14:59.089
They are really good.

00:14:59.089 --> 00:15:02.690
And I'm not only saying this because you are
my guest.

00:15:02.690 --> 00:15:09.620
I actually did mostly use your write-ups to
learn about this stuff, which actually did

00:15:09.620 --> 00:15:17.890
lead to the XSS, let's say, in Golang's HTML
library, which didn't use, which wasn't as

00:15:17.890 --> 00:15:23.989
advanced as those things, but I'm happy that
I've learned something.

00:15:23.989 --> 00:15:28.899
But I know that you didn't start your bug
hunting career or your web security career

00:15:28.899 --> 00:15:34.500
by finding these, let's say, state-of-the-art
XSSs.

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:36.910
I think you started with the very simple ones.

00:15:36.910 --> 00:15:43.019
So can you tell me what was the first bug
you ever reported?

00:15:43.019 --> 00:15:53.560
I mean, when it comes to my bug hunting career,
the first bug I reported was an XSS in Google

00:15:53.560 --> 00:15:57.230
Blogger, and it was actually discarded.

00:15:57.230 --> 00:16:06.149
I mean, I didn't get any work for that because
the attack scenario was that I needed to send

00:16:06.149 --> 00:16:09.730
a zip file to the victim and the victim needed
to upload it themselves.

00:16:09.730 --> 00:16:17.079
So, yeah, I mean, I think I agree that, like,
if I can make a victim do that, then I would

00:16:17.079 --> 00:16:22.100
just put something else in the zip file that,
you know, take over their computer.

00:16:22.100 --> 00:16:24.470
Why bother with that XSS in that case?

00:16:24.470 --> 00:16:32.480
Yeah, so that was the first bug I reported
in Bug Bounty, but I think I reported some

00:16:32.480 --> 00:16:38.630
bugs before, just, like, you know, casually
I found something in some library.

00:16:38.630 --> 00:16:46.240
I think, like, my first part, like, the first
report that I did for some external software

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:56.290
was to PHPMyAdmin, and I think that was in
2011, maybe, something like that.

00:16:56.290 --> 00:17:06.240
That was completely, you know, inexploitable
XSS, but there was a bug that I might, you

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:11.220
know, misremember the details, but it was
something like that, that if you use, like,

00:17:11.220 --> 00:17:22.010
order by in your SQL query, then in the result,
the PHPMyAdmin would say that this is a result

00:17:22.010 --> 00:17:30.591
from to, and if you put an SQL comment in
that, then it would just, you know, reflect

00:17:30.591 --> 00:17:31.740
that SQL comment.

00:17:31.740 --> 00:17:38.050
So, if you put, like, a snippet of HTML in
the comment, then it would just, you had an

00:17:38.050 --> 00:17:44.650
XSS in that case, but it was inexploitable
because that was basically, that was like

00:17:44.650 --> 00:17:51.710
a self-XSS, pretty much, because it has, like,
C-SERV token and all stuff like that, but

00:17:51.710 --> 00:17:55.530
the other was a bug that just didn't escape
data as they should, or maybe they should

00:17:55.530 --> 00:17:57.720
cast it to an integer.

00:17:57.720 --> 00:18:00.190
I think that's what they ended up doing.

00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:07.539
Yeah, but, you know, I was thinking if I should
report that or not, because I knew it wasn't

00:18:07.539 --> 00:18:12.390
explainable, but then I, I mean, I found that
maybe they would say that, okay, it doesn't

00:18:12.390 --> 00:18:13.390
matter.

00:18:13.390 --> 00:18:15.840
I also didn't really brag about this report.

00:18:15.840 --> 00:18:21.030
I don't think I ever put that even on my Twitter
or anything.

00:18:21.030 --> 00:18:27.809
There is a CVE for that, which I think is
pretty funny, because, yeah, but, you know,

00:18:27.809 --> 00:18:33.650
I think that, like, recently there's been
a discussion about the usefulness of CVEs,

00:18:33.650 --> 00:18:41.510
and, like, somebody reported on SQL injection
in the vulnerable web application, so, yeah,

00:18:41.510 --> 00:18:44.010
CVEs are pretty useless sometimes.

00:18:44.010 --> 00:18:50.150
But, yeah, I got CVE from that, even though,
but, you know, I didn't brag about it, because

00:18:50.150 --> 00:18:53.950
it wasn't anything special or anything exploitable.

00:18:53.950 --> 00:19:02.400
Yeah, I think a lot of us just start with
bugs that are not exploitable, and then as

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:08.100
we go, we learn about the attack scenario
and how important this is, that it's something

00:19:08.100 --> 00:19:09.530
practical.

00:19:09.530 --> 00:19:15.190
Can you tell me how did you then transition,
let's say, from self-exercises into ones that

00:19:15.190 --> 00:19:16.190
were rewarded?

00:19:16.190 --> 00:19:30.289
Yeah, I mean, I think that my work as a pentester
was very, I mean, yeah, so the work as a pentester

00:19:30.289 --> 00:19:37.929
gave me really much here, because I was talking
to clients constantly, and oftentimes we had

00:19:37.929 --> 00:19:46.710
discussions whether a bug is exploitable and
whether we should consider this bug a security

00:19:46.710 --> 00:19:51.380
issue, if it's, like, a high severity or medium
severity or stuff like that.

00:19:51.380 --> 00:20:01.140
So, like, the discussions with customers made
me, you know, think more about the attack

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:02.140
scenarios.

00:20:02.140 --> 00:20:09.440
And by the way, I actually am thinking about
hosting a video on my YouTube channel about

00:20:09.440 --> 00:20:16.190
that, about the attack scenarios and when
an attack makes sense, when it doesn't make

00:20:16.190 --> 00:20:17.210
sense.

00:20:17.210 --> 00:20:27.549
But I think, like, during my pentesting career,
like in Securitum, I was a person who tended

00:20:27.549 --> 00:20:39.380
to lower the severity of bugs, like the most
of all people, because, yeah, I remember,

00:20:39.380 --> 00:20:45.510
I think I can, I won't share any specific
details, but, like, some general idea.

00:20:45.510 --> 00:20:51.390
I remember that I was testing some application
in, like, a pentest of an application, and

00:20:51.390 --> 00:20:57.559
I found a SQL injection, and at that point,
in my head, SQL injection was pretty much

00:20:57.559 --> 00:21:01.000
an automatic, you know, high severity or critical
severity.

00:21:01.000 --> 00:21:11.010
But, okay, I tried to exploit it further,
and this was a SQL injection in an insurance

00:21:11.010 --> 00:21:14.230
application, and you could insure your car
in that.

00:21:14.230 --> 00:21:22.450
So, it turns out that the database contained
only data about cars, so, you know, models,

00:21:22.450 --> 00:21:25.409
manufacturers, types of engines, and stuff
like that.

00:21:25.409 --> 00:21:30.390
There was absolutely no data of users.

00:21:30.390 --> 00:21:35.350
And, you know, when I reported a bug, and,
like, automatically, I gave it, like, high

00:21:35.350 --> 00:21:41.929
severity, but before sending this report,
I just, you know, stopped for a while, and

00:21:41.929 --> 00:21:49.770
I was just thinking to myself, what if the
customer comes back and asks me, why is this

00:21:49.770 --> 00:21:51.400
the high severity?

00:21:51.400 --> 00:21:57.980
And then I realized that I can't convince
myself that this is high severity, because

00:21:57.980 --> 00:22:01.350
I didn't, you know, gather anything from that.

00:22:01.350 --> 00:22:05.950
I, everything that was in the database was
public data anyway.

00:22:05.950 --> 00:22:11.590
So, yeah, I remember that was the first time
that I know the severity of SQL injection.

00:22:11.590 --> 00:22:14.390
I don't remember if it was medium or too low.

00:22:14.390 --> 00:22:15.390
What was that?

00:22:15.390 --> 00:22:23.960
But it wasn't, like, high severity, because
it's just, you know, I couldn't, how can I

00:22:23.960 --> 00:22:30.750
convince somebody else that it is high severity
if I cannot convince myself?

00:22:30.750 --> 00:22:38.490
I think that was a pretty, pretty important
moment for me, because it made me realize

00:22:38.490 --> 00:22:44.210
that you cannot just have an automatic assignment
that this type of vulnerability is this type

00:22:44.210 --> 00:22:47.280
of severity.

00:22:47.280 --> 00:22:53.950
You know, all depends on many different factors,
like how, what kind of data you can access,

00:22:53.950 --> 00:22:56.960
how likely the attack, like, I don't know.

00:22:56.960 --> 00:23:04.280
I guess that some people consider, like, start
XSS as more severe than reflected XSS, but

00:23:04.280 --> 00:23:07.350
it also doesn't have to be true in all cases.

00:23:07.350 --> 00:23:13.760
There might be cases where reflected XSS will
actually be more likely to execute than started

00:23:13.760 --> 00:23:14.970
XSS.

00:23:14.970 --> 00:23:20.980
Like, if there is, like, a super common application,
like Google Docs, for instance, and if you

00:23:20.980 --> 00:23:27.110
have a reflected XSS in Google Docs, then,
you know, it's very, very easy to mount an

00:23:27.110 --> 00:23:28.110
attack.

00:23:28.110 --> 00:23:33.310
But if you have a started XSS in a web interface
of some network device that the admin visits

00:23:33.310 --> 00:23:40.950
once a year, then, you know, XSS might never
actually get executed.

00:23:40.950 --> 00:23:50.090
So, yeah, I think that during my pentesting
job, and, you know, clients keep talking about

00:23:50.090 --> 00:23:56.260
that they just like to not agree with the,
you know, assignments of severity.

00:23:56.260 --> 00:24:01.450
And when it happens over and over again, then
you start thinking about it more.

00:24:01.450 --> 00:24:08.350
You kind of like to prepare and to think about
what they might say, what might be their arguments,

00:24:08.350 --> 00:24:13.419
what they might not like about this vulnerability,
about this scenario.

00:24:13.419 --> 00:24:21.430
Oftentimes, it's difficult to, you know, as
a pentester, you only see a fragment of the

00:24:21.430 --> 00:24:22.650
whole ecosystem.

00:24:22.650 --> 00:24:26.169
You might not understand how everything is
connected.

00:24:26.169 --> 00:24:32.880
Because right now, I was talking that we might
consider a vulnerability a high severity,

00:24:32.880 --> 00:24:35.150
but the client might consider it lower.

00:24:35.150 --> 00:24:38.200
But sometimes, it was actually the other way
around.

00:24:38.200 --> 00:24:46.299
Sometimes, we didn't consider something very
severe, but the client knew how it interacted

00:24:46.299 --> 00:24:48.640
with some different parts of the system.

00:24:48.640 --> 00:24:54.760
Or maybe that we didn't consider something
serious, but the client knew that doing some

00:24:54.760 --> 00:24:57.980
stuff actually generates some costs.

00:24:57.980 --> 00:25:02.580
And if you can do that, like without limits,
then it's a pretty serious problem for them.

00:25:02.580 --> 00:25:07.250
Then, you know, it also can happen this way
around.

00:25:07.250 --> 00:25:15.429
So, yeah, like over all of these years, I
really like whether an attack scenario is

00:25:15.429 --> 00:25:19.590
viable, whether it makes sense.

00:25:19.590 --> 00:25:26.010
And I always like to think about the preconditions
for an attack.

00:25:26.010 --> 00:25:33.890
So, you know, the main question is, if we
consider an attack scenario, and we know that

00:25:33.890 --> 00:25:40.120
the attacker has to be in a certain place
to mount this attack, are there other attacks

00:25:40.120 --> 00:25:46.000
that are much, much simpler than that that
they can do, given this, you know, precondition?

00:25:46.000 --> 00:25:48.149
It's like I said with the XSS in Blogger.

00:25:48.149 --> 00:25:51.700
That was my first XSS report in Google Backbone.

00:25:51.700 --> 00:25:59.890
Like, if we can make somebody take a zip file
from us and do something with that, then why

00:25:59.890 --> 00:26:05.059
would we bother with an XSS in, you know,
a Blogger admin interface?

00:26:05.059 --> 00:26:13.750
We would probably be able to match more severe
stuff without having to have any, you know,

00:26:13.750 --> 00:26:15.059
vulnerability in the application itself.

00:26:15.059 --> 00:26:20.380
You maybe could just make them run DevTools
and do something in that, or maybe just run

00:26:20.380 --> 00:26:23.789
an executable from the zip file.

00:26:23.789 --> 00:26:29.530
So, yeah, that's the change that happened.

00:26:29.530 --> 00:26:31.830
But I think it just comes with experience.

00:26:31.830 --> 00:26:36.520
You see how people react, what it imparts,
and then you understand also their point of

00:26:36.520 --> 00:26:37.570
view.

00:26:37.570 --> 00:26:43.299
That not every vulnerability that you find
is something that the world needs to stop

00:26:43.299 --> 00:26:44.480
to fix that.

00:26:44.480 --> 00:26:50.330
And there might actually be vulnerabilities
that people might acknowledge, but they might

00:26:50.330 --> 00:26:55.540
decide that they don't want to fix that right
now or maybe for the next couple of months.

00:26:55.540 --> 00:27:01.160
And it can also make sense when you keep the
threat model in mind.

00:27:01.160 --> 00:27:08.340
Yeah, I think I started paying more attention
to the actual impact in the sort of bug bounty

00:27:08.340 --> 00:27:10.490
world than in the pentesting world.

00:27:10.490 --> 00:27:18.779
In pentesting, I feel like there was more
using of pre-written templates of bug descriptions

00:27:18.779 --> 00:27:22.980
and things like that, because when you report,
you know, 10, 15, 20 vulnerabilities in one

00:27:22.980 --> 00:27:24.010
week...

00:27:24.010 --> 00:27:26.390
By the way, if I can interrupt, I actually
hated that.

00:27:26.390 --> 00:27:29.480
I mean, I didn't use template descriptions.

00:27:29.480 --> 00:27:31.190
I hated doing that.

00:27:31.190 --> 00:27:32.190
Really?

00:27:32.190 --> 00:27:33.190
Yeah.

00:27:33.190 --> 00:27:37.490
Would you write a personal description of
every single bug in pentest?

00:27:37.490 --> 00:27:40.159
I mean, yes.

00:27:40.159 --> 00:27:41.390
For most of them, yes.

00:27:41.390 --> 00:27:42.529
Like, only for...

00:27:42.529 --> 00:27:43.529
Wow.

00:27:43.529 --> 00:27:47.769
Like, if I have an exercise, then I would
always write a report, like, what you can

00:27:47.769 --> 00:27:51.110
do in the context of this application with
this exercise.

00:27:51.110 --> 00:27:52.980
Okay, that's nice.

00:27:52.980 --> 00:27:53.980
I didn't...

00:27:53.980 --> 00:27:56.919
Well, now in the bug bounty, I obviously have
to do it.

00:27:56.919 --> 00:27:59.850
In pentesting, I admit I didn't.

00:27:59.850 --> 00:28:04.840
And only, you know, in bug bounty, where you
have to convince the other side in every single

00:28:04.840 --> 00:28:10.950
report that you have an actual impact, it
taught me to also start paying attention to

00:28:10.950 --> 00:28:12.750
this during pentests.

00:28:12.750 --> 00:28:13.750
Yeah.

00:28:13.750 --> 00:28:16.080
Back when I worked there.

00:28:16.080 --> 00:28:17.630
Yeah, I think that's...

00:28:17.630 --> 00:28:18.720
Yeah, I mean...

00:28:18.720 --> 00:28:20.400
Yeah, I think that's good.

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:21.400
Yeah.

00:28:21.400 --> 00:28:24.970
We have also some, like, pentesting clients
who just accepted everything.

00:28:24.970 --> 00:28:28.480
Whatever we write, they're okay with that.

00:28:28.480 --> 00:28:32.730
But, yeah.

00:28:32.730 --> 00:28:38.590
We had, of course, we had at Techuricum some,
you know, pre-written templates for, like,

00:28:38.590 --> 00:28:41.330
many, many types of bugs.

00:28:41.330 --> 00:28:46.169
But, I mean, I've used some of them.

00:28:46.169 --> 00:28:48.130
But, I don't know.

00:28:48.130 --> 00:28:54.490
There's something in me that I just don't
like to use template descriptions.

00:28:54.490 --> 00:28:59.520
I want to make it, you know, more specific
to the application.

00:28:59.520 --> 00:29:01.870
Of course, it also depended on the scale.

00:29:01.870 --> 00:29:05.960
Because if I had, like, if there was, like,
an every possible bug in the application in

00:29:05.960 --> 00:29:11.240
every possible place, it also happens, then
it probably doesn't make that much of a sense

00:29:11.240 --> 00:29:13.210
to describe everything.

00:29:13.210 --> 00:29:15.659
But, yeah.

00:29:15.659 --> 00:29:22.180
Yeah, what I did, like, if I had, like, many
exercises in a single application, that's

00:29:22.180 --> 00:29:24.310
also an interesting question.

00:29:24.310 --> 00:29:30.430
Like, if you find the same vulnerability in
different places, would you report it as a

00:29:30.430 --> 00:29:32.820
one bug with, like, different locations?

00:29:32.820 --> 00:29:36.650
Or would you report it as a few bugs?

00:29:36.650 --> 00:29:41.710
And, as usual, it depends.

00:29:41.710 --> 00:29:42.990
Yeah.

00:29:42.990 --> 00:29:50.750
Like, my approach was that, of course, when
I do path testing or bug bounty, it's usually

00:29:50.750 --> 00:29:51.750
black box.

00:29:51.750 --> 00:29:53.090
So, I cannot see the source code.

00:29:53.090 --> 00:29:55.190
I don't know the exact root cause.

00:29:55.190 --> 00:30:05.029
But, sometimes, you can see if you have a
few instances of probably the same bug.

00:30:05.029 --> 00:30:12.610
And, when I assume that this is the case,
that, okay, this is, like, different locations,

00:30:12.610 --> 00:30:14.039
different parameters, different endpoints.

00:30:14.039 --> 00:30:19.500
But, the root cause is probably the same that
I reported it as a one bug.

00:30:19.500 --> 00:30:27.690
But, if I thought that there are several different
root causes, then I would report this as distinct

00:30:27.690 --> 00:30:28.690
bugs.

00:30:28.690 --> 00:30:38.000
And, the main reason that's also really interesting,
I think, that in the path testing, we often

00:30:38.000 --> 00:30:39.240
do retests.

00:30:39.240 --> 00:30:47.830
So, yeah, we report bugs, the customer fixes
those bugs, and we need to do retests.

00:30:47.830 --> 00:30:56.809
And, if I report, like, two different XSSs
in, you know, one bug, and then I do a retest,

00:30:56.809 --> 00:31:03.360
and one is fixed, and one is not fixed, then
it's more difficult to state that in the report.

00:31:03.360 --> 00:31:05.700
You need to say that it's partially fixed.

00:31:05.700 --> 00:31:06.990
It's pretty bad.

00:31:06.990 --> 00:31:10.830
I mean, you can do that, of course, but I
didn't like that.

00:31:10.830 --> 00:31:15.289
So, if you have two separate issues, then
you can say, okay, this one is fixed, this

00:31:15.289 --> 00:31:16.289
one is not fixed.

00:31:16.289 --> 00:31:17.289
It's pretty obvious.

00:31:17.289 --> 00:31:23.940
But, if you report many different things in
one bug, and they fix some of them, but, like,

00:31:23.940 --> 00:31:30.399
not all of them, then it starts being not
that, you know, it's just not easy to grasp

00:31:30.399 --> 00:31:33.200
what exactly it means, like, partially fixed.

00:31:33.200 --> 00:31:37.480
You need to state exactly these endpoints
are fixed, these endpoints are not fixed,

00:31:37.480 --> 00:31:43.539
and then it starts being, you basically kind
of start reporting this as a separate bug,

00:31:43.539 --> 00:31:44.539
but in one bug.

00:31:44.539 --> 00:31:48.649
You need to, you know, make your description
much longer.

00:31:48.649 --> 00:31:51.570
So, yeah.

00:31:51.570 --> 00:32:00.429
And, speaking of doing read tests, it also
taught me one very, I believe it's a very

00:32:00.429 --> 00:32:05.000
important skill, is writing proof of concepts.

00:32:05.000 --> 00:32:07.330
And, I have a nice story here.

00:32:07.330 --> 00:32:14.399
It happened in the beginning of my career
as a pen tester, was that I did a pen test

00:32:14.399 --> 00:32:24.520
of some application, and the client came back
with a read test, I think, a year later.

00:32:24.520 --> 00:32:29.000
So, you don't remember about this application
anything, basically.

00:32:29.000 --> 00:32:34.539
And then, I had, you know, proof of concept
or the description of the attack in the report,

00:32:34.539 --> 00:32:36.900
and I wasn't able to replicate that.

00:32:36.900 --> 00:32:40.220
It was too fake.

00:32:40.220 --> 00:32:42.720
It was too bad to be able to, you know.

00:32:42.720 --> 00:32:47.559
If you don't remember details, then you can't
do that from the description alone.

00:32:47.559 --> 00:32:54.320
And, I spent a really long time trying to,
you know, remember what exactly I was doing,

00:32:54.320 --> 00:32:58.480
how I was able to get to this place to mount
this attack.

00:32:58.480 --> 00:33:04.830
And, yeah, that taught me that from, like,
that point, my proof of concepts are really

00:33:04.830 --> 00:33:05.830
step by step.

00:33:05.830 --> 00:33:09.850
You need to click here, you need to go here,
you need to make sure that you are logged

00:33:09.850 --> 00:33:14.370
in as an account with this and that privileges
and stuff like that.

00:33:14.370 --> 00:33:22.350
And, yeah, basically, my reason for writing
better proof of concept was that I should

00:33:22.350 --> 00:33:26.630
be able to, you know, reproduce that back
from this proof of concept.

00:33:26.630 --> 00:33:35.769
It should be, or maybe to put it another way,
if you have a person who never saw this application

00:33:35.769 --> 00:33:40.140
before, then that person should be able to
reproduce the back based on your proof of

00:33:40.140 --> 00:33:41.140
concept.

00:33:41.140 --> 00:33:42.140
That was, like, my line of thinking.

00:33:42.140 --> 00:33:49.010
Yeah, and that's good for bug bounty because
sometimes we think that we report the back

00:33:49.010 --> 00:33:51.780
to someone that knows the application really
well.

00:33:51.780 --> 00:33:57.809
But the truth is that often it's triaged by
a person who is responsible for many different

00:33:57.809 --> 00:34:02.680
bug bounty programs, and they do not know
anything about the application you are reporting

00:34:02.680 --> 00:34:03.680
the back currently.

00:34:03.680 --> 00:34:11.040
So, I also, I don't know who told this where,
but I remember a tip from someone to actually

00:34:11.040 --> 00:34:18.720
include things like creating an account, logging
in, using proper settings, setup or whatever

00:34:18.720 --> 00:34:21.869
and only then doing the proof of concept.

00:34:21.869 --> 00:34:28.359
And I also see, I'm not a triager, obviously,
but when I do the case studies, sometimes

00:34:28.359 --> 00:34:35.310
I study a lot of, like, tens or hundreds of
bug bounty reports of a particular bug class.

00:34:35.310 --> 00:34:41.169
And so, obviously, I want to, and I extract
some information about the report, and I see

00:34:41.169 --> 00:34:48.589
that if there is a good proof of concept,
like step-by-step reproduction, is the quickest

00:34:48.589 --> 00:34:52.700
way for me to, like, understand, okay, what
is this report about, what's the impact and

00:34:52.700 --> 00:34:53.700
things like that.

00:34:53.700 --> 00:34:58.359
And then I often don't even need to take a
look at the description and what they wrote

00:34:58.359 --> 00:35:05.210
about the impact, because the good proof of
concept is a replacement of anything.

00:35:05.210 --> 00:35:07.490
Yeah, that's right.

00:35:07.490 --> 00:35:14.160
Also, yeah, also in Google, for instance,
like there are also some people on triage

00:35:14.160 --> 00:35:19.110
when it comes to the VRP, Vulnerability Reward
Program.

00:35:19.110 --> 00:35:25.450
And, you know, Google has like thousands of
applications, literally, and there are like,

00:35:25.450 --> 00:35:28.089
I don't know, 50 people on triage.

00:35:28.089 --> 00:35:31.730
So, there is no way that they know every single
application.

00:35:31.730 --> 00:35:38.610
And also, I think, like, a good way of thinking
about it is that if you are a bug hunter and

00:35:38.610 --> 00:35:45.320
you spend a lot of time on a single application,
then it's really, really likely that you know

00:35:45.320 --> 00:35:49.040
more about this application than any person
from the triage.

00:35:49.040 --> 00:35:50.050
Yeah.

00:35:50.050 --> 00:35:55.599
So, keep, like, write as many details as you
possibly can.

00:35:55.599 --> 00:35:58.420
You will make life easier for the people in
triage.

00:35:58.420 --> 00:36:07.170
It will also make it easier to, you know,
grade your report and to understand what the

00:36:07.170 --> 00:36:08.950
real impact is.

00:36:08.950 --> 00:36:11.950
Yeah, especially when the bug is complex.

00:36:11.950 --> 00:36:17.839
And speaking about the complexity, when I
look at your bugs from like 2013, 14, maybe

00:36:17.839 --> 00:36:23.390
even 15, those exercises are often quite simple.

00:36:23.390 --> 00:36:29.230
And then you started getting deeper into this
and reporting the more advanced exercises,

00:36:29.230 --> 00:36:35.620
the mutation exercises, the crazy stuff a
lot of us don't even know about.

00:36:35.620 --> 00:36:42.450
How did this transition happen and how did
you learn those more advanced things?

00:36:42.450 --> 00:36:48.910
Yeah, that's an interesting question because
I don't think I have a good answer for that,

00:36:48.910 --> 00:36:50.660
to be honest.

00:36:50.660 --> 00:36:52.830
I think it just came.

00:36:52.830 --> 00:36:59.720
I mean, one thing that truly happened was
that my approach in the beginning of my bug

00:36:59.720 --> 00:37:05.630
hunting career was that I was trying to find
old applications.

00:37:05.630 --> 00:37:12.260
Basically, I was using some Google hacking
and, yeah, Google hacking on Google and trying

00:37:12.260 --> 00:37:15.930
to find, like, forgotten old application on
Google.

00:37:15.930 --> 00:37:16.930
And it was possible.

00:37:16.930 --> 00:37:19.329
There were, like, many, many of them.

00:37:19.329 --> 00:37:23.290
And at some point, I was out of ideas.

00:37:23.290 --> 00:37:25.950
Like, what else can I do?

00:37:25.950 --> 00:37:29.440
At some point, you run out of ideas.

00:37:29.440 --> 00:37:37.700
And, yeah, that was the point when I started
to just dig deeper into the application that

00:37:37.700 --> 00:37:41.270
I know to find some bugs.

00:37:41.270 --> 00:37:49.510
And also, another factor was I think it was
in 2015 or maybe 16 that Google introduced

00:37:49.510 --> 00:37:51.100
research grants.

00:37:51.100 --> 00:37:58.450
And the idea of research grants was that they
gave money upfront to bug hunters to specifically

00:37:58.450 --> 00:38:01.520
look at certain applications.

00:38:01.520 --> 00:38:07.760
So, of course, if you report a bug, you get
a normal reward, but the grant was supposed

00:38:07.760 --> 00:38:11.500
to motivate you to look for some application.

00:38:11.500 --> 00:38:18.430
And I think it really worked for me because
I looked at some applications, like, you know,

00:38:18.430 --> 00:38:23.560
very, very carefully, digging very, very deep.

00:38:23.560 --> 00:38:30.660
And I tried to understand exactly what happens,
yeah, what happens inside.

00:38:30.660 --> 00:38:37.260
And I knew that I won't probably get some
easy XSS, like, you know, you could just get

00:38:37.260 --> 00:38:39.390
parameter and you have an easy XSS.

00:38:39.390 --> 00:38:46.839
So, I needed to understand what exactly happened
in the application, how it transforms, processes

00:38:46.839 --> 00:38:51.859
the input, what are the sources, what are
the things.

00:38:51.859 --> 00:38:58.990
So, yeah, but I don't have, like, a good answer
to, like, how exactly the transition happened.

00:38:58.990 --> 00:39:04.030
I believe that it's just something that comes
with experience.

00:39:04.030 --> 00:39:10.560
And, you know, when you report some simple
stuff, you also maybe at some point get kind

00:39:10.560 --> 00:39:13.890
of bored of that and you want to do something
more interesting.

00:39:13.890 --> 00:39:20.589
And, yeah, you start looking at, like, different
stuff.

00:39:20.589 --> 00:39:31.119
Also, I think that for me, yeah, like, many
people, like, during the trainings that I

00:39:31.119 --> 00:39:34.730
gave in my previous company, we will probably
talk about it a little bit later.

00:39:34.730 --> 00:39:35.730
Yeah.

00:39:35.730 --> 00:39:40.869
But many people ask me what is my methodology
for finding security bugs in Bug Bounty or

00:39:40.869 --> 00:39:42.150
in PathOS.

00:39:42.150 --> 00:39:48.240
And I didn't like the question because, you
know, some people have, like, some kind of

00:39:48.240 --> 00:39:49.240
methodologies.

00:39:49.240 --> 00:39:52.410
They like to do this and that and that.

00:39:52.410 --> 00:39:57.600
Maybe they use some other references like
OS or whatnot.

00:39:57.600 --> 00:40:04.980
But I'm the kind of person that uses my punch
and I don't have any specific methodology.

00:40:04.980 --> 00:40:09.569
I'm just, you know, clicking randomly on the
application, seeing what it does.

00:40:09.569 --> 00:40:12.960
I'm trying to understand what kind of library
it uses.

00:40:12.960 --> 00:40:17.640
And, yeah, I just go with what my punch says.

00:40:17.640 --> 00:40:23.250
And I know it's pretty bad from the, you know,
educational perspective because it doesn't

00:40:23.250 --> 00:40:26.220
give any practical advice to anyone.

00:40:26.220 --> 00:40:31.230
But, yeah, that's actually how I approach.

00:40:31.230 --> 00:40:38.329
I just look at the application and I try to
see something specific in this application,

00:40:38.329 --> 00:40:41.319
what can be a potential issue.

00:40:41.319 --> 00:40:44.290
And, yeah, that actually works for me.

00:40:44.290 --> 00:40:45.290
Yeah.

00:40:45.290 --> 00:40:46.290
What tools do you use for this?

00:40:46.290 --> 00:40:49.530
Is it mostly developer tools inside the browser?

00:40:49.530 --> 00:40:50.530
Yeah.

00:40:50.530 --> 00:41:01.920
When it comes to the client side issues, then
yes, I use DevTools in Chromium.

00:41:01.920 --> 00:41:05.609
And let me think.

00:41:05.609 --> 00:41:11.060
Yeah, I think that's pretty much it when it
comes to Chromium.

00:41:11.060 --> 00:41:15.390
I sometimes write some scripts for myself
or some browser's extension.

00:41:15.390 --> 00:41:17.510
What do they do?

00:41:17.510 --> 00:41:18.510
Sorry?

00:41:18.510 --> 00:41:24.090
What do those scripts do?

00:41:24.090 --> 00:41:31.369
They usually do something automatically that
would be to deduce manually, like some kind

00:41:31.369 --> 00:41:40.050
of transformation or like one of the things
that I actually made public was a script for

00:41:40.050 --> 00:41:42.520
finding prototype pollution gadgets.

00:41:42.520 --> 00:41:51.751
So it took a snippet of JavaScript and it
transformed it in such a way that if the script

00:41:51.751 --> 00:41:58.380
is trying to access a non-existing property,
that it is a possible gadget for, you know,

00:41:58.380 --> 00:42:00.490
client side prototype pollution.

00:42:00.490 --> 00:42:01.490
Yeah.

00:42:01.490 --> 00:42:05.710
Sometimes I'm just adding some logging because
I'm just interested in, you know, what happens

00:42:05.710 --> 00:42:08.599
exactly in the application.

00:42:08.599 --> 00:42:11.569
But in most cases, that was like pretty...

00:42:11.569 --> 00:42:18.359
So basically just things to sort of speed
up the feedback loop of what happens.

00:42:18.359 --> 00:42:23.950
Do you often debug the JavaScript inside the
browser or do you rather statically read it?

00:42:23.950 --> 00:42:25.300
Yeah.

00:42:25.300 --> 00:42:27.930
I'm a big proponent of dynamic analysis.

00:42:27.930 --> 00:42:34.140
I think that's my main tool, how I work with
the code.

00:42:34.140 --> 00:42:38.420
There are pretty nice tricks actually in the
Chromium DevTools.

00:42:38.420 --> 00:42:40.210
I believe are not widely known.

00:42:40.210 --> 00:42:45.609
Like, for instance, that there is a function
called debug that you can use in the DevTools

00:42:45.609 --> 00:42:47.730
and you can pretty much debug everything.

00:42:47.730 --> 00:42:53.250
You can even debug a setter on inner HTML
or something like that.

00:42:53.250 --> 00:42:58.460
And I really, really like to use that, you
know, to find the piece of code that is interesting

00:42:58.460 --> 00:42:59.470
for me quickly.

00:42:59.470 --> 00:43:09.530
There are also features like, you know, practically
everybody who used debugger knows that you

00:43:09.530 --> 00:43:12.609
can set a breakpoint in the DevTools.

00:43:12.609 --> 00:43:16.140
But you can also set like a conditional breakpoint.

00:43:16.140 --> 00:43:20.880
So the breakpoints that fired only in certain
conditions are met.

00:43:20.880 --> 00:43:25.150
But also you can have, I think it's called
lockpoint.

00:43:25.150 --> 00:43:29.650
So it is not a breakpoint, but you can, you
know, just do a constant lock in this line

00:43:29.650 --> 00:43:32.210
whenever this kind of code is called.

00:43:32.210 --> 00:43:37.760
So your code is not stopped because sometimes
it is pretty annoying, especially when the

00:43:37.760 --> 00:43:40.079
application is doing like a lot of stuff.

00:43:40.079 --> 00:43:45.150
For instance, if you try to set a breakpoint
on postMessage in any Google application,

00:43:45.150 --> 00:43:49.470
then your debugger will stop like every single
second.

00:43:49.470 --> 00:43:51.300
Just not usable at all.

00:43:51.300 --> 00:43:52.300
Yeah.

00:43:52.300 --> 00:43:53.300
Yeah.

00:43:53.300 --> 00:43:55.770
So this kind of tricks is what I'm using.

00:43:55.770 --> 00:44:03.580
I also, I don't remember why exactly I did
that, but I remember that at one point I also

00:44:03.580 --> 00:44:08.119
used the Chromium, I mean, I used the Puppeteer,
it's called.

00:44:08.119 --> 00:44:12.060
I think the Node.js library for controlling
Chromium.

00:44:12.060 --> 00:44:13.079
Yeah.

00:44:13.079 --> 00:44:18.760
Because, I don't know, the application was
doing something annoying and I wasn't able

00:44:18.760 --> 00:44:20.369
to work with that.

00:44:20.369 --> 00:44:29.460
And Puppeteer, I was just intercepting the
JavaScript and I was changing something in

00:44:29.460 --> 00:44:30.460
the JavaScript.

00:44:30.460 --> 00:44:32.349
So it's not that annoying.

00:44:32.349 --> 00:44:33.750
Okay.

00:44:33.750 --> 00:44:38.390
I don't remember the details, but I remember
I did something like that.

00:44:38.390 --> 00:44:39.390
Okay.

00:44:39.390 --> 00:44:46.780
This use case I didn't know about, but for
our listeners, in BBR review, I just finished

00:44:46.780 --> 00:44:52.849
a four-part series about DevTools and all
the things that Michal described here are

00:44:52.849 --> 00:44:53.849
there.

00:44:53.849 --> 00:44:56.700
So you may want to check this out.

00:44:56.700 --> 00:45:02.579
One thing I learned while writing those articles
and while writing the docs is that you can

00:45:02.579 --> 00:45:05.720
reset frames while debugging.

00:45:05.720 --> 00:45:11.150
So you can run over the same function over
and over again, which I didn't know about

00:45:11.150 --> 00:45:15.809
and it also speeds up the work massively.

00:45:15.809 --> 00:45:17.510
Yeah.

00:45:17.510 --> 00:45:25.510
Another trick that I like to use is, for instance,
assume that you test an application that uses

00:45:25.510 --> 00:45:33.910
some kind of HTML sanitizer and sometimes
it is pretty annoying to do that directly

00:45:33.910 --> 00:45:38.280
from the application because, I don't know,
you need to pick some check boxes or you need

00:45:38.280 --> 00:45:42.530
to press a button every single time and stuff
like that.

00:45:42.530 --> 00:45:48.790
So I also like to find places that JavaScript
code does sanitization.

00:45:48.790 --> 00:45:52.530
The assumption here is that it's client-side
sanitization.

00:45:52.530 --> 00:45:59.280
And if you can get a reference function, like
in a debugger, because the nice thing about

00:45:59.280 --> 00:46:05.150
using the debugger in Chromium or in any other
browser is that if you execute a command in

00:46:05.150 --> 00:46:09.319
the console, then you are in the context of
where you are currently stopped.

00:46:09.319 --> 00:46:12.540
So you can use the lock-on variables and stuff
like that.

00:46:12.540 --> 00:46:18.330
So if I can find the method which performs
the sanitization, then I can just go to the

00:46:18.330 --> 00:46:22.710
console and call it directly without doing
all this user interface stuff.

00:46:22.710 --> 00:46:24.650
Yeah, that's a good tip.

00:46:24.650 --> 00:46:28.630
How would you find this function?

00:46:28.630 --> 00:46:32.119
Yeah, that's an interesting question.

00:46:32.119 --> 00:46:34.710
There might be several ways to do that.

00:46:34.710 --> 00:46:43.000
I might assume that if the application is
doing HTML sanitization, then it uses a browser

00:46:43.000 --> 00:46:47.930
provided HTML parser, such as like the DOM
parser parsing string.

00:46:47.930 --> 00:46:51.849
So I might try to set a breakpoint on that.

00:46:51.849 --> 00:47:07.579
I might try to set a breakpoint on setter
of innerHTML.

00:47:07.579 --> 00:47:13.170
If you know that the sanitized HTML is put
somewhere in the DOM tree, then in DevTools

00:47:13.170 --> 00:47:18.680
it is also possible to set a breakpoint whenever
a DOM node changes.

00:47:18.680 --> 00:47:24.099
So you can set a breakpoint on a certain DOM
node.

00:47:24.099 --> 00:47:27.069
For instance, if add a child appended.

00:47:27.069 --> 00:47:29.540
So then you can do that.

00:47:29.540 --> 00:47:33.829
If you know that this is where the sanitized
HTML ends up.

00:47:33.829 --> 00:47:39.829
And then you can go up through the stack trace
to find the place.

00:47:39.829 --> 00:47:48.039
Because if you know that it is out here, then
somewhere near, hopefully, this generates.

00:47:48.039 --> 00:47:55.569
So I focus on either the sink or the source.

00:47:55.569 --> 00:47:57.849
So I'm trying to think.

00:47:57.849 --> 00:48:00.130
Sometimes you can assume that there is a sanitizer.

00:48:00.130 --> 00:48:02.559
You can just look for the word script.

00:48:02.559 --> 00:48:09.240
Because if there is one tag that every single
sanitizer strips out, then it's script.

00:48:09.240 --> 00:48:14.900
So you might be able to find it just by looking
for some strings that you assume that the

00:48:14.900 --> 00:48:17.200
sanitizer will have.

00:48:17.200 --> 00:48:21.630
Yeah, that's a good tip right here.

00:48:21.630 --> 00:48:26.590
And what are your tips for reading?

00:48:26.590 --> 00:48:34.849
Because I saw also some of your work was that
you found out new stuff in the browser or

00:48:34.849 --> 00:48:38.180
new ways to define something in the browser.

00:48:38.180 --> 00:48:43.950
How do you get knowledge about those changes
in the browser?

00:48:43.950 --> 00:48:47.869
Basically from Twitter or X.

00:48:47.869 --> 00:48:48.869
Okay.

00:48:48.869 --> 00:48:56.840
I follow people who, I mean, among people
that I follow, there are also people that

00:48:56.840 --> 00:49:04.609
work in browsers, like in Chromium, in the
Safari team, or in the Firefox team.

00:49:04.609 --> 00:49:09.530
And when something new happens, then they
like to tweet about it.

00:49:09.530 --> 00:49:17.501
There are also some Twitter accounts, like,
I don't remember the names, but something

00:49:17.501 --> 00:49:20.380
like Intents2, I think it's called.

00:49:20.380 --> 00:49:26.210
Because there is a process in all browsers
that if they want to introduce a new feature,

00:49:26.210 --> 00:49:31.910
then they do something like Intents2 experiment,
Intents2 shape, stuff like that.

00:49:31.910 --> 00:49:36.450
So there is some kind of documentation that
they want to introduce something new.

00:49:36.450 --> 00:49:44.589
And then you can get the information about
that from the sources.

00:49:44.589 --> 00:49:46.590
But sometimes these are just random tweets.

00:49:46.590 --> 00:49:55.720
I remember that a few years ago, I did a security
analysis of Portal Elements.

00:49:55.720 --> 00:50:01.819
And the inspiration from that was from a tweet
by Frederick Brown, which, by the way, is

00:50:01.819 --> 00:50:04.040
in the Firefox team.

00:50:04.040 --> 00:50:09.260
And that was a tweet, something along the
lines of, hey, you have a specification for

00:50:09.260 --> 00:50:13.200
the Portal Element, but it doesn't have any
security consideration.

00:50:13.200 --> 00:50:18.230
And I thought, ah, that's interesting, because,
I mean, the security considerations might

00:50:18.230 --> 00:50:23.890
be empty, because this specification doesn't
really relate to security.

00:50:23.890 --> 00:50:26.339
That might be the case.

00:50:26.339 --> 00:50:30.300
But also, maybe nobody really thought about
the security consideration.

00:50:30.300 --> 00:50:38.630
And, yeah, I believe the latter was the case
in Portal, unfortunately for me.

00:50:38.630 --> 00:50:41.410
What happened later with the Portal Element?

00:50:41.410 --> 00:50:42.869
I'm sorry?

00:50:42.869 --> 00:50:47.520
What happened later with your research on
the Portal Element?

00:50:47.520 --> 00:50:50.380
What happened after the question?

00:50:50.380 --> 00:50:58.720
Because I think I saw your bug using the Portal
Element, the same margin policy bypass.

00:50:58.720 --> 00:51:00.200
Yeah.

00:51:00.200 --> 00:51:04.869
How did you get from reading the tweet into
finding the same margin policy bypass?

00:51:04.869 --> 00:51:07.180
I see.

00:51:07.180 --> 00:51:20.100
So, yeah, I read the spec, and I was just
trying to understand why do we even need portals?

00:51:20.100 --> 00:51:22.920
What does it even do?

00:51:22.920 --> 00:51:26.470
And it is similar to iframe.

00:51:26.470 --> 00:51:30.930
That was the first thing that was easy to
notice.

00:51:30.930 --> 00:51:32.680
But it's not exactly iframe.

00:51:32.680 --> 00:51:39.830
The main difference was that in Portal Element,
you could call a method.

00:51:39.830 --> 00:51:42.349
I think it was called activate.

00:51:42.349 --> 00:51:48.540
And then the Portal Element is, like, upgraded
to be in the top frame, like, instantaneously,

00:51:48.540 --> 00:51:50.650
without any load.

00:51:50.650 --> 00:51:56.549
So, yeah, so, you know, the first thing that
I could notice was that, okay, it is similar

00:51:56.549 --> 00:51:58.849
to iframe.

00:51:58.849 --> 00:52:07.369
And in iframe, one thing that you can do is
that you can set the source attribute to,

00:52:07.369 --> 00:52:09.880
like, JavaScript one.

00:52:09.880 --> 00:52:15.930
So, yeah, I was trying to do the same thing
on Portal, and it worked.

00:52:15.930 --> 00:52:21.799
But the question was, in what context does
the JavaScript execute?

00:52:21.799 --> 00:52:30.320
And it turned out that in Portal, the JavaScript
executed in context of the page that was already

00:52:30.320 --> 00:52:31.320
loaded.

00:52:31.320 --> 00:52:37.569
It was pretty much equivalent to what happens
if you just, you know, paste the JavaScript

00:52:37.569 --> 00:52:38.750
into the address bar.

00:52:38.750 --> 00:52:45.530
So, if you enter the JavaScript and JavaScript
column and stuff, then it executes in the

00:52:45.530 --> 00:52:47.400
currently loaded page.

00:52:47.400 --> 00:52:53.260
And if you try to navigate Portal, then it
was pretty much equivalent to enter some stuff

00:52:53.260 --> 00:52:54.260
in the address bar.

00:52:54.260 --> 00:53:02.140
So, if you were on, like, accounts.google.com,
that was my example, and you could, you know,

00:53:02.140 --> 00:53:04.980
enter JavaScript in the address bar.

00:53:04.980 --> 00:53:10.930
If I tried to navigate Portal to JavaScript,
then it executed in the context of accounts.google.com.

00:53:10.930 --> 00:53:21.050
But, you know, when I got the idea that this
is equivalent to entering a URL in the address

00:53:21.050 --> 00:53:27.040
bar, then the natural next step for me was
that, okay, but in the address bar, you can

00:53:27.040 --> 00:53:29.230
also navigate to a Pyle URL.

00:53:29.230 --> 00:53:31.880
You cannot do that in iframe.

00:53:31.880 --> 00:53:36.580
I mean, you cannot do that in iframe if you
are in the HTTPS protocol.

00:53:36.580 --> 00:53:41.890
Because this is, like, it is not possible
to navigate to local resources from HTTPS.

00:53:41.890 --> 00:53:46.430
But in Portal, when I tried to navigate to
Pyle, it worked.

00:53:46.430 --> 00:53:50.839
It really showed the Pyle in the Portal.

00:53:50.839 --> 00:53:57.130
So, what you could do was that you could navigate
to Pyle URL, like, etc password, and then

00:53:57.130 --> 00:54:01.119
you can navigate to JavaScript to get the
content of that etc password.

00:54:01.119 --> 00:54:09.700
So, that was not only same-origin policy bypass,
but it was also reading correct files, which

00:54:09.700 --> 00:54:11.920
is even worse.

00:54:11.920 --> 00:54:18.470
But, yeah, I think that this bug was, I think
it was kind of obvious.

00:54:18.470 --> 00:54:21.880
I mean, in a way that it was the really natural
thing to check.

00:54:21.880 --> 00:54:23.799
Okay, it is similar to iframe.

00:54:23.799 --> 00:54:26.560
In iframe, this thing is blocked.

00:54:26.560 --> 00:54:30.020
Let's see if it is blocked in a Portal.

00:54:30.020 --> 00:54:31.990
I need one.

00:54:31.990 --> 00:54:34.870
And that's pretty much the story about Portal.

00:54:34.870 --> 00:54:38.480
I found, like, five or six bugs, I think.

00:54:38.480 --> 00:54:41.430
And most of them were like, okay, this is
normally blocked.

00:54:41.430 --> 00:54:45.730
The question is, is that blocked in Portal?

00:54:45.730 --> 00:54:49.120
It's a bug report.

00:54:49.120 --> 00:54:54.640
Do you know if the Portal element, because
back then it was in experimental mode or something

00:54:54.640 --> 00:54:55.640
like this in Chromium?

00:54:55.640 --> 00:54:56.640
I think it still is.

00:54:56.640 --> 00:55:00.780
I don't think it's actually used.

00:55:00.780 --> 00:55:12.069
I mean, they obviously fixed those bugs, but
yeah, I think it's also not under the enable

00:55:12.069 --> 00:55:16.599
experimental web platform features, but it's
under a separate flag, like enable Portal.

00:55:16.599 --> 00:55:17.599
Okay.

00:55:17.599 --> 00:55:20.180
So even less people have that.

00:55:20.180 --> 00:55:25.890
There's also an opposition from other browsers.

00:55:25.890 --> 00:55:31.390
So I'm not really sure what is the status
of Portals.

00:55:31.390 --> 00:55:36.700
I know that there were many, many, many security
issues because, like, I found the security

00:55:36.700 --> 00:55:37.930
issues.

00:55:37.930 --> 00:55:44.420
Some other people, when they found out that
something called Portal even exists, they

00:55:44.420 --> 00:55:46.900
started to look for other issues.

00:55:46.900 --> 00:55:51.819
And I know that there were several other,
like, memory corruption issues in Portals

00:55:51.819 --> 00:55:53.119
as well.

00:55:53.119 --> 00:55:57.869
So it was really a goldmine for bug hunters.

00:55:57.869 --> 00:56:02.569
Does Google pay normally for features that
are experimental or they have, like, lower

00:56:02.569 --> 00:56:05.500
bounty table for them?

00:56:05.500 --> 00:56:15.740
I'm not exactly sure what the policy currently
is, to be honest.

00:56:15.740 --> 00:56:24.770
I think the policy changed a few times in
the meantime, and I'm not sure if the bugs

00:56:24.770 --> 00:56:27.079
would be rewarded today, to be honest.

00:56:27.079 --> 00:56:31.280
Maybe they would, maybe they wouldn't.

00:56:31.280 --> 00:56:33.480
I don't know what is the policy.

00:56:33.480 --> 00:56:34.480
Okay.

00:56:34.480 --> 00:56:41.460
And also, this is the bug in the browser,
not really in any website.

00:56:41.460 --> 00:56:48.520
And it's also a shift that was present in
your career.

00:56:48.520 --> 00:56:57.880
What was your drive to looking at bugs from
just bugs in normal websites to bugs in browsers?

00:56:57.880 --> 00:57:07.109
Yeah, as I mentioned, when it comes to bug
hunting, I focused pretty much on the Google

00:57:07.109 --> 00:57:09.350
web application.

00:57:09.350 --> 00:57:18.500
And at some point, it started to be more and
more difficult to find bugs in, like, exercises

00:57:18.500 --> 00:57:24.220
of the client-side issues and also the server-side
issues in Google applications that are not

00:57:24.220 --> 00:57:27.430
like acquisitions, because acquisitions have
many bugs.

00:57:27.430 --> 00:57:31.599
But the rewards for acquisitions are much
lower.

00:57:31.599 --> 00:57:37.030
And yeah, I was interested in, you know, Google.com
domain-based.

00:57:37.030 --> 00:57:43.390
And at some point, it started to be more and
more difficult.

00:57:43.390 --> 00:57:53.260
And, you know, they, my feeling, my punch
was that there might be more bugs, but there

00:57:53.260 --> 00:57:57.780
might be difficult to find using black box
approach.

00:57:57.780 --> 00:58:03.640
And the nice thing about looking for bugs
in browsers is that all browsers are open

00:58:03.640 --> 00:58:04.640
source.

00:58:04.640 --> 00:58:06.970
I mean, all the engines are open source.

00:58:06.970 --> 00:58:13.400
And if you believe that you are onto something,
and you can just look at the source code,

00:58:13.400 --> 00:58:16.540
you can find what exactly happens.

00:58:16.540 --> 00:58:21.880
It's not always easy to find, like, exactly
what part of the source code is responsible

00:58:21.880 --> 00:58:24.589
for the functions you are currently testing.

00:58:24.589 --> 00:58:31.960
But, you know, the easiest way is that if
you have any error message, then it's usually

00:58:31.960 --> 00:58:36.569
the easiest way to find by the message placed
in the code.

00:58:36.569 --> 00:58:43.339
But, yeah, I think that the portal element
was actually the first thing that I did that

00:58:43.339 --> 00:58:45.950
was related directly to the browser.

00:58:45.950 --> 00:58:50.869
And I believe that it actually motivated me
to look more.

00:58:50.869 --> 00:58:55.539
So one thing was that it was more and more
difficult to find bugs in web applications.

00:58:55.539 --> 00:59:02.789
The other thing was that, you know, you have
source code of the browsers, but also it just

00:59:02.789 --> 00:59:09.210
didn't know before that when I tried to find
some bugs in a browser mechanism, then I was

00:59:09.210 --> 00:59:10.210
able to do that.

00:59:10.210 --> 00:59:14.950
That was a really interesting lesson for me.

00:59:14.950 --> 00:59:25.599
By the way, I think that generally my lesson,
like during my whole life, is that you might

00:59:25.599 --> 00:59:30.740
have some assumptions, whether you are good
at something, but the assumption might be

00:59:30.740 --> 00:59:31.740
wrong.

00:59:31.740 --> 00:59:35.859
I mean, you might think that you like doing
something, but when you do that, more than

00:59:35.859 --> 00:59:39.450
you started to see that, okay, it's not actually
what you like.

00:59:39.450 --> 00:59:41.900
And the other way around, you might not.

00:59:41.900 --> 00:59:45.289
Because I think that before portals, I didn't
even think about testing browsers.

00:59:45.289 --> 00:59:49.720
But, okay, I tried that and it went well.

00:59:49.720 --> 00:59:57.670
In the beginning of my pentesting career,
I thought that the most interesting area of

00:59:57.670 --> 01:00:00.630
pentesting is the infrastructure.

01:00:00.630 --> 01:00:05.780
I remember that I saw some presentations about
Metasploit and stuff like that, and I thought

01:00:05.780 --> 01:00:11.910
it's super, super interesting, and I want
to do infrastructure pentesting.

01:00:11.910 --> 01:00:14.762
And I did, a little bit.

01:00:14.762 --> 01:00:21.290
But it just happened that I was better at
doing web, basically.

01:00:21.290 --> 01:00:27.380
So, you know, at some point I decided that
I don't think that I want to do the infrastructure

01:00:27.380 --> 01:00:34.730
anymore, at least not much of it, and I want
to focus more and more on web, because it

01:00:34.730 --> 01:00:37.700
just happens that I'm better at it.

01:00:37.700 --> 01:00:47.270
And I think that's a nice place to share also
a fun story, how my interest in security in

01:00:47.270 --> 01:00:51.720
general started, because it was an infrastructure
thing.

01:00:51.720 --> 01:00:58.809
I think I was in high school at that point.

01:00:58.809 --> 01:01:01.200
I already knew a little bit of programming.

01:01:01.200 --> 01:01:03.730
I programmed in Python and Visual Basic.

01:01:03.730 --> 01:01:07.910
That was actually my first programming language.

01:01:07.910 --> 01:01:14.960
And I knew some stuff around, but networking
was like pure magic for me.

01:01:14.960 --> 01:01:18.589
I didn't know at all how networking works.

01:01:18.589 --> 01:01:29.160
And one day, like randomly, I found an article
on Wikipedia about ARP spoofing.

01:01:29.160 --> 01:01:34.491
And, you know, I always assume that what happens
in the network is like some magical, super

01:01:34.491 --> 01:01:35.869
advanced algorithm.

01:01:35.869 --> 01:01:42.720
But then you read how the ARP protocol works,
and it's like super, super simple, super naive.

01:01:42.720 --> 01:01:45.280
And then you see how the attack works.

01:01:45.280 --> 01:01:48.710
And the attack is like super easy in theory.

01:01:48.710 --> 01:01:56.950
Basically, for those of you who don't know,
the basic idea of the ARP spoofing attack

01:01:56.950 --> 01:02:02.550
is that imagine that you are standing in front
of a group of people, and you are asking,

01:02:02.550 --> 01:02:03.640
who is John?

01:02:03.640 --> 01:02:06.940
And you assume that the John will arise.

01:02:06.940 --> 01:02:12.770
But somebody else might rise their hand.

01:02:12.770 --> 01:02:18.010
And then you give something that was supposed
to go to John to that person.

01:02:18.010 --> 01:02:20.390
And this is pretty much how it works in networking.

01:02:20.390 --> 01:02:28.299
We trust that when we ask, who is this IT
address or this MAC address, then we trust

01:02:28.299 --> 01:02:31.950
that the right machine responds.

01:02:31.950 --> 01:02:35.760
And then I learned about a tool.

01:02:35.760 --> 01:02:38.190
I don't remember how it's called.

01:02:38.190 --> 01:02:40.430
Anyway, it doesn't matter.

01:02:40.430 --> 01:02:47.470
But there was a tool with which you could
mount the ARP spoofing.

01:02:47.470 --> 01:02:50.099
And I tried that in my local network.

01:02:50.099 --> 01:02:54.500
And, you know, it was in a year before like
HTTPS everywhere.

01:02:54.500 --> 01:03:00.380
So it was pretty nice that I could tell my
brother, hey, go to this website, and suddenly

01:03:00.380 --> 01:03:02.119
some other text showed up.

01:03:02.119 --> 01:03:07.990
And that was pretty much my first proof of
concept that I did of an attack.

01:03:07.990 --> 01:03:14.849
That, you know, some random text changed on
some pages because everything was under HTTP.

01:03:14.849 --> 01:03:21.660
And, yeah, at that point, that was really
a turning point for me.

01:03:21.660 --> 01:03:25.130
Because, hey, the network is not pure magic.

01:03:25.130 --> 01:03:26.910
It's actually pretty simple.

01:03:26.910 --> 01:03:29.380
At least some of the protocols are simple.

01:03:29.380 --> 01:03:31.890
Not all of them, of course.

01:03:31.890 --> 01:03:35.290
But, yeah, so it all started with infrastructure
things.

01:03:35.290 --> 01:03:39.180
And now I'm not doing any infrastructure at
all.

01:03:39.180 --> 01:03:44.680
To be honest, when I started learning about
security, I also wasn't attracted to web security

01:03:44.680 --> 01:03:46.410
at all.

01:03:46.410 --> 01:03:50.789
I don't really know why, but I thought I want
to do like reverse engineering or something

01:03:50.789 --> 01:03:52.650
in this direction.

01:03:52.650 --> 01:03:57.140
And I think I was just afraid of complexity
of the web.

01:03:57.140 --> 01:03:59.950
And then I just started learning it for some
reason.

01:03:59.950 --> 01:04:03.100
And I got into it.

01:04:03.100 --> 01:04:06.520
And apparently it works well.

01:04:06.520 --> 01:04:12.880
There's also a good book called, I think,
Be So Good They Can't Ignore You.

01:04:12.880 --> 01:04:21.650
And it really touches upon the fact that to
like something, you just have to become good

01:04:21.650 --> 01:04:23.020
enough at something.

01:04:23.020 --> 01:04:27.910
And then you sort of start liking it.

01:04:27.910 --> 01:04:34.450
And it's against people that are saying that
you have some natural abilities for one profession

01:04:34.450 --> 01:04:35.980
only.

01:04:35.980 --> 01:04:38.490
And I think the book is really good.

01:04:38.490 --> 01:04:41.299
I can recommend it to anyone.

01:04:41.299 --> 01:04:46.990
But the takeaway is just when you are getting
good at something, then you sort of start

01:04:46.990 --> 01:04:48.299
liking it naturally.

01:04:48.299 --> 01:04:51.329
Obviously, that's not always the case.

01:04:51.329 --> 01:04:53.839
I do think that people have some predispositions.

01:04:53.839 --> 01:04:59.539
Are they more analytical or more relationship
oriented or whatever?

01:04:59.539 --> 01:05:08.839
But within such a space as security where
it's pretty much similar skill set, I think

01:05:08.839 --> 01:05:15.510
for most people, they could like a lot of
different branches of security.

01:05:15.510 --> 01:05:22.460
And they could jump around and learn pretty
much anything.

01:05:22.460 --> 01:05:33.559
The nice thing is also that if you know one
area of security well, then it's easier to

01:05:33.559 --> 01:05:41.440
jump into another one because you have some
background and you understand what it's all

01:05:41.440 --> 01:05:42.440
about a little bit better.

01:05:42.440 --> 01:05:49.810
I mean, if I was supposed to do, I don't know,
a Linux exploit development, then I would

01:05:49.810 --> 01:05:51.980
start pretty much from zero.

01:05:51.980 --> 01:05:58.550
But I think given some of the background that
I have, it would be easier for me to jump

01:05:58.550 --> 01:06:04.160
into that topic than to a person who didn't
have any experience with security at all.

01:06:04.160 --> 01:06:06.900
Yeah, 100%.

01:06:06.900 --> 01:06:13.740
So yeah, that's also nice that all of this
experience that you have can also be useful.

01:06:13.740 --> 01:06:20.970
Or maybe if you have a different kind of background
than other people working in this field, that

01:06:20.970 --> 01:06:29.170
could also be useful because this background
might come useful in certain situations.

01:06:29.170 --> 01:06:30.680
You mentioned reverse engineering.

01:06:30.680 --> 01:06:36.829
So if you are a reverse engineer with good
knowledge about web, it can come useful if

01:06:36.829 --> 01:06:42.329
you happen to reverse engineer something related
to web in some way that the other people might

01:06:42.329 --> 01:06:44.850
not be very familiar with.

01:06:44.850 --> 01:06:50.720
And speaking of learning, I have never found
a prototype pollution bug.

01:06:50.720 --> 01:06:55.470
And what would be your tips for me to find
one?

01:06:55.470 --> 01:06:59.000
Yeah, that's an interesting question.

01:06:59.000 --> 01:07:03.410
Because in Bug Bounty, I also didn't report
a single prototype pollution bug.

01:07:03.410 --> 01:07:10.319
So that's also a little bit of a shift.

01:07:10.319 --> 01:07:20.420
At some point, I started sharing more research
stuff instead of just bugs.

01:07:20.420 --> 01:07:23.529
And I just found prototype pollution interesting.

01:07:23.529 --> 01:07:28.620
I mean, okay, we might say that I reported
some bugs, but it wasn't in Bug Bounty.

01:07:28.620 --> 01:07:32.640
It was like, I don't know, I think I reported
that to Don't Purify.

01:07:32.640 --> 01:07:35.710
How about the Jibana RCE?

01:07:35.710 --> 01:07:40.660
Yeah, that's also an interesting story.

01:07:40.660 --> 01:07:44.390
That's a bug that wasn't found by me.

01:07:44.390 --> 01:07:47.220
It was just found by somebody else.

01:07:47.220 --> 01:07:50.570
I don't know even by who, to be honest.

01:07:50.570 --> 01:07:55.630
The story here was that there was a training
organized by Securiton.

01:07:55.630 --> 01:08:05.990
There was one participant that said that he
wants to understand prototype pollution better.

01:08:05.990 --> 01:08:12.839
And he found this Jibana vulnerability that
doesn't have a public proof of concept.

01:08:12.839 --> 01:08:19.989
And he was able to find what kind of module
has this vulnerability.

01:08:19.989 --> 01:08:26.429
And yeah, we can look at that and maybe we
can find some kind of scenario.

01:08:26.429 --> 01:08:33.350
So yeah, that's also something that's happened
a lot in my career.

01:08:33.350 --> 01:08:36.900
That some things happen by accident, pretty
much.

01:08:36.900 --> 01:08:46.630
Because if it wasn't for that one participant
of the training, then maybe I would have dived

01:08:46.630 --> 01:08:49.509
into prototype pollution a little bit later.

01:08:49.509 --> 01:08:51.880
But yeah, that was the motivation.

01:08:51.880 --> 01:08:58.909
But anyway, prototype pollution was more of
a research project for me.

01:08:58.909 --> 01:09:01.760
I just found it interesting.

01:09:01.760 --> 01:09:08.819
I believe that this is not a bug that is much
talked about.

01:09:08.819 --> 01:09:15.819
And it was just interesting for me from purely
technical perspective.

01:09:15.819 --> 01:09:25.240
And yeah, I was just trying to understand
how viable this attack scenario is.

01:09:25.240 --> 01:09:30.489
And of course, I did try to test some Google
properties for prototype pollution.

01:09:30.489 --> 01:09:35.949
But I wasn't able to find any bugs.

01:09:35.949 --> 01:09:42.770
Yeah, but when it comes to tips, I think there
is a really good research.

01:09:42.770 --> 01:09:48.500
I think it was called just something like
exploiting client-side prototype pollution.

01:09:48.500 --> 01:09:50.430
Something like that.

01:09:50.430 --> 01:09:57.949
And they shared some techniques, how they
found the sources for prototype pollution.

01:09:57.949 --> 01:10:04.380
Like if you are looking for client-side prototype
pollution, then you can just randomly add

01:10:04.380 --> 01:10:08.400
a proto parameters to get the bones to JSON
everywhere.

01:10:08.400 --> 01:10:18.650
And you can just look into your browser dev
tools to see if you can see your properties

01:10:18.650 --> 01:10:20.469
on object prototype.

01:10:20.469 --> 01:10:28.900
I think that the way Chromium sorts the properties,
it's like a side effect of an implementation,

01:10:28.900 --> 01:10:31.699
is that the new properties are always in the
beginning.

01:10:31.699 --> 01:10:37.230
So if you look like an object prototype, then
your new properties should be at the beginning.

01:10:37.230 --> 01:10:42.489
I'm not 100% sure that it happens, but I think
that was the case.

01:10:42.489 --> 01:10:50.590
I also think that the BURP suite has some
features, some new features related to prototype

01:10:50.590 --> 01:10:56.120
pollution, but I didn't have much chance to
test that.

01:10:56.120 --> 01:11:02.580
There is the DOM invader, which is like a
browser extension actually.

01:11:02.580 --> 01:11:05.770
And I absolutely love this extension for DOM
access.

01:11:05.770 --> 01:11:10.850
And it also has some features to find prototype
pollution, but the prototype pollution features

01:11:10.850 --> 01:11:12.510
I haven't tested actually.

01:11:12.510 --> 01:11:17.280
Now I think I probably should turn it on and
see what comes.

01:11:17.280 --> 01:11:22.830
Yeah, that might be interesting to see what
kind of issues you can find.

01:11:22.830 --> 01:11:24.020
Yeah.

01:11:24.020 --> 01:11:29.480
And speaking of trainings, what kinds of trainings
did you lead and for who?

01:11:29.480 --> 01:11:30.820
Yeah.

01:11:30.820 --> 01:11:38.780
So during my last two years at Securitium,
I was really focused on giving trainings,

01:11:38.780 --> 01:11:43.500
public talks, basically on education.

01:11:43.500 --> 01:11:48.970
And so I had many different trainings.

01:11:48.970 --> 01:11:54.219
The most popular one was called Web of Infinite
Security.

01:11:54.219 --> 01:12:02.290
And it was a two-day workshop about SQL injection,
XSS, authorization issues, authentication

01:12:02.290 --> 01:12:07.300
issues, server-side request forgery, and stuff
like that.

01:12:07.300 --> 01:12:16.449
And we just taught people about security issues
they can expect in web applications by showing

01:12:16.449 --> 01:12:21.989
them how they can be exploited.

01:12:21.989 --> 01:12:28.620
The participants got a training license for
the web, so they could work with that themselves.

01:12:28.620 --> 01:12:39.280
And yeah, the basic idea, the usual target
for these trainings were developers to try

01:12:39.280 --> 01:12:45.949
to teach them about web security and what
kind of security issues they can expect.

01:12:45.949 --> 01:12:56.920
And yeah, I remember that in the beginning,
I kind of expected that when people leave

01:12:56.920 --> 01:13:03.330
the training, then they should just write
super secure code after that, because they

01:13:03.330 --> 01:13:08.660
learned everything that they need to know
about at least the basic security stuff.

01:13:08.660 --> 01:13:15.320
But at some point, I understood that this
is not what happens.

01:13:15.320 --> 01:13:24.010
And I considered a success of my training
if a person can recognize a security issue.

01:13:24.010 --> 01:13:30.949
Like when they write a code, then they can,
you know, have suddenly a light bulb above

01:13:30.949 --> 01:13:36.159
their head that, hey, I think there might
be something wrong with this code.

01:13:36.159 --> 01:13:45.290
So my goal was to build some kind of intuition,
because if we talk about some specific security

01:13:45.290 --> 01:13:50.010
issues, then we can also try to generalize
them a little bit.

01:13:50.010 --> 01:13:57.989
And yeah, I would consider a success if a
person, when writing a code, thinks twice,

01:13:57.989 --> 01:14:01.770
hey, this probably is something that might
be not safe.

01:14:01.770 --> 01:14:08.000
But also something that happened a few times
during the training was that after the first

01:14:08.000 --> 01:14:11.410
day of the training, people started to test
their own applications.

01:14:11.410 --> 01:14:18.250
And on the second day, we started the training,
and they said, hey, we found this bug in our

01:14:18.250 --> 01:14:19.430
application.

01:14:19.430 --> 01:14:23.790
That was actually super cool, and it's one
of the best things that could happen on the

01:14:23.790 --> 01:14:30.690
training, because immediately you know that
for the people, it was ready to go to the

01:14:30.690 --> 01:14:31.690
training.

01:14:31.690 --> 01:14:32.690
Yeah.

01:14:32.690 --> 01:14:40.770
What did you see them getting wrong the most
often about security?

01:14:40.770 --> 01:14:48.950
I think the understanding of attack scenarios,
the stuff that we talked about before.

01:14:48.950 --> 01:14:54.929
Because one, an example, one of the arguments
that was brought a few times was that when

01:14:54.929 --> 01:15:02.530
I say that we have client-side HTML sanitizers,
and that you probably want to use client-side

01:15:02.530 --> 01:15:08.690
HTML sanitizers, because you use exactly the
same HTML parser that is used by your browser,

01:15:08.690 --> 01:15:10.510
so it makes sense.

01:15:10.510 --> 01:15:16.170
And then some people would argue that, okay,
but if it's client-side sanitizer, then you

01:15:16.170 --> 01:15:18.610
can disable it or stuff like that.

01:15:18.610 --> 01:15:21.490
If it happens on the client, how we can trust
it?

01:15:21.490 --> 01:15:32.610
And yeah, that was the part when you needed
to describe what is the threat model.

01:15:32.610 --> 01:15:37.820
And I believe that it is widely, it is really
widely, widely misunderstood.

01:15:37.820 --> 01:15:42.210
I mean, it's not easy if you don't really
also, if you don't think about it that much,

01:15:42.210 --> 01:15:44.139
and it's really easy to misunderstand.

01:15:44.139 --> 01:15:51.170
But I also had a training that was called
front-end security training, and it was about

01:15:51.170 --> 01:15:52.170
XSS.

01:15:52.170 --> 01:15:55.760
Basically, the whole first day was about XSS,
and the second day was about other front-end

01:15:55.760 --> 01:15:57.860
issues.

01:15:57.860 --> 01:16:05.409
And yeah, the thing that I was trying to,
I was hoping for the people to understand

01:16:05.409 --> 01:16:10.929
was that the attack scenario here, basically
for most front-end issues, is that you send

01:16:10.929 --> 01:16:14.650
a link to someone, and then you can do something.

01:16:14.650 --> 01:16:20.400
Because if we start talking that you can do
something in the dev tools of the browsers,

01:16:20.400 --> 01:16:28.070
or that you can, I don't know, intercept the
HTTPS traffic, in theory you can do that.

01:16:28.070 --> 01:16:34.000
If you have some impossible supercomputer
or you find a bug in the algorithm, you can

01:16:34.000 --> 01:16:35.000
do that.

01:16:35.000 --> 01:16:39.290
But this is not something that we care about
in context of issues like XSS.

01:16:39.290 --> 01:16:43.080
It's just completely outside of the threat
model.

01:16:43.080 --> 01:16:54.300
And it is very tricky how to describe that
to people so that they understand.

01:16:54.300 --> 01:17:03.320
Even HTTPS, which is the most widely deployed
security mechanism in the world, there were

01:17:03.320 --> 01:17:12.400
people who asked, if the site had HTTPS, why
did they have a security breach?

01:17:12.400 --> 01:17:21.330
And we might laugh about it a little bit,
but it's actually a really tricky educational

01:17:21.330 --> 01:17:22.330
issue.

01:17:22.330 --> 01:17:29.100
How can we make people understand what they
are protecting against when they use HTTPS

01:17:29.100 --> 01:17:32.800
or any other security mechanism?

01:17:32.800 --> 01:17:40.270
Another good example, I believe, is that if
you have an encrypted messenger on your WhatsApp

01:17:40.270 --> 01:17:45.860
or Signal or whatever, end-to-end encryption,
nobody can read the messages.

01:17:45.860 --> 01:17:52.920
But if you leave your phone unlocked on the
table, it doesn't matter if the communication

01:17:52.920 --> 01:18:00.690
is encrypted end-to-end because anyone who
can just take the phone can read the messages.

01:18:00.690 --> 01:18:07.580
And sometimes in the internet you can see
some comments or discussions, hey, they use

01:18:07.580 --> 01:18:09.389
Signal, it has end-to-end encryption.

01:18:09.389 --> 01:18:14.030
How the government organization was able to
decrypt the message?

01:18:14.030 --> 01:18:17.500
And they probably didn't even have to decrypt
anything.

01:18:17.500 --> 01:18:22.800
They probably had direct access to the screen.

01:18:22.800 --> 01:18:28.870
So it's really, really tricky because we have
many, many different mechanisms that prevent

01:18:28.870 --> 01:18:31.830
some specific attacks.

01:18:31.830 --> 01:18:41.380
And people who, I mean, I believe that people
should have some general understanding about

01:18:41.380 --> 01:18:42.639
security.

01:18:42.639 --> 01:18:49.670
By the way, I really think that it is something
that should be taught in school, you know,

01:18:49.670 --> 01:18:54.510
in the informatics lessons, like some basics.

01:18:54.510 --> 01:19:02.640
I mean, I don't know what is currently being
taught at school, maybe it is, actually.

01:19:02.640 --> 01:19:10.630
So people should have some general understanding,
but I can understand that they do need to

01:19:10.630 --> 01:19:20.190
know that HTTPS also keeps the integrity of
the data because when you talk about HTTPS

01:19:20.190 --> 01:19:24.900
you usually think that it encrypts, but it's
not all it does, actually.

01:19:24.900 --> 01:19:31.360
And it probably doesn't matter for most people
to also check the integrity of the data, but

01:19:31.360 --> 01:19:34.449
it is actually, from the cryptographic perspective,
super, super important.

01:19:34.449 --> 01:19:40.250
If it wouldn't do that, then it would be a
really bad protocol, to be honest.

01:19:40.250 --> 01:19:47.909
So, yeah, that was tricky because if you get
a question about, you know, security, about

01:19:47.909 --> 01:19:57.219
the threat model, about the attack scenario,
it's very tricky to answer that question,

01:19:57.219 --> 01:20:02.030
to describe it, without some browser context.

01:20:02.030 --> 01:20:07.510
And, yeah, of course there are, like, other
things that people get wrong, like I don't

01:20:07.510 --> 01:20:08.600
know how to use BERT.

01:20:08.600 --> 01:20:13.530
I mean, I love BERT, but let's be honest,
user interface is not the best in the world,

01:20:13.530 --> 01:20:16.900
especially when you need to use it for the
first time.

01:20:16.900 --> 01:20:22.690
But that's something that you can just learn,
but the attack scenario, the threat modeling,

01:20:22.690 --> 01:20:28.670
is something that you need to understand to
kind of get that, what's it all about.

01:20:28.670 --> 01:20:37.480
So, yeah, I think it mainly came up in the
front-end training that, you know, stuff like,

01:20:37.480 --> 01:20:43.680
does it make sense to have some kind of security
mechanism in the browser?

01:20:43.680 --> 01:20:46.960
Because, like, you can bypass everything that
happens in the browser.

01:20:46.960 --> 01:20:49.050
That was the argument.

01:20:49.050 --> 01:20:52.219
Yeah, you can.

01:20:52.219 --> 01:20:59.280
I mean, the argument that I typically use
is that just because you can do something

01:20:59.280 --> 01:21:04.030
on your own computer, it doesn't mean that
it's easy to do on somebody else's computer.

01:21:04.030 --> 01:21:07.560
So you can run DevTools in your own browser
and you can delete everything, you can change

01:21:07.560 --> 01:21:13.810
the code as much as you like, but it doesn't
mean that it's easy to do to someone else.

01:21:13.810 --> 01:21:21.600
And I think that's the shortest answer to
this question, but, yeah, it's really interesting

01:21:21.600 --> 01:21:23.880
to, you know, dig deeper.

01:21:23.880 --> 01:21:28.900
And it's also very relevant, you know, to
path testing and to back-binding reports to

01:21:28.900 --> 01:21:37.050
also understand the threat models and if something
is a vulnerability or how severe of a vulnerability

01:21:37.050 --> 01:21:38.050
that is.

01:21:38.050 --> 01:21:45.469
Yeah, I think also from my experience, I don't
do trainings now, but I used to, and I think

01:21:45.469 --> 01:21:51.440
server-side vulnerabilities are so much easier
to explain to people than client-side stuff.

01:21:51.440 --> 01:21:56.770
Because with XSS, you have the same modern
policy, you have different types of XSSs.

01:21:56.770 --> 01:22:05.290
Like, for example, when you have an XSS in
the uploaded file, it's really hard to explain

01:22:05.290 --> 01:22:10.250
to the person that if it is a sandbox domain,
it's not a risk, but if it's a normal domain,

01:22:10.250 --> 01:22:14.159
it is a risk, even though there is nothing
on the page, but you can do the request.

01:22:14.159 --> 01:22:20.520
There's so much complexity there that I really
think XSS, even though it's the most popular

01:22:20.520 --> 01:22:27.040
vulnerability type, I think, from the web
vulnerabilities, it is super complex and super

01:22:27.040 --> 01:22:28.250
misunderstood.

01:22:28.250 --> 01:22:30.100
Yeah, that's right.

01:22:30.100 --> 01:22:37.510
I also think that the typical proof-of-concept
for XSS also doesn't do it any favors because

01:22:37.510 --> 01:22:44.360
people always see the alert one, they don't
have any idea what you can realistically do.

01:22:44.360 --> 01:22:47.620
That's basically what I started with my trainings.

01:22:47.620 --> 01:22:53.610
Before even saying what XSS exactly is, how
we can exploit that, I was just showing a

01:22:53.610 --> 01:22:57.460
real example that you can get some data.

01:22:57.460 --> 01:23:04.179
Like, I had a demonstration that you could
take over an admin account in WordPress thanks

01:23:04.179 --> 01:23:05.179
to XSS.

01:23:05.179 --> 01:23:09.781
I started with that and I was just saying
that from now on, you will see mostly the

01:23:09.781 --> 01:23:15.520
alert one, but keep in mind that instead of
the alert one, we could do all the stuff that

01:23:15.520 --> 01:23:18.880
I just showed.

01:23:18.880 --> 01:23:20.550
Yeah.

01:23:20.550 --> 01:23:26.710
I think it was all new for many people, so
on the second day, some of the people kind

01:23:26.710 --> 01:23:33.699
of forgot about that and asked again, like,
okay, but yeah, I have that, but I can't do

01:23:33.699 --> 01:23:35.170
alert one, so what?

01:23:35.170 --> 01:23:38.550
Yeah, but I also learned over...

01:23:38.550 --> 01:23:47.230
I mean, I remember that when I started doing
trainings, I think it was in 2014 or 15, I

01:23:47.230 --> 01:24:00.920
assumed that people will know more about security
in general, and I thought that the most stuff

01:24:00.920 --> 01:24:07.050
that are widely known, that there actually
are, and like, for instance, something that

01:24:07.050 --> 01:24:14.190
kind of surprised me, but it doesn't surprise
me anymore, is JSPack, which is a way to write

01:24:14.190 --> 01:24:16.400
JavaScript code.

01:24:16.400 --> 01:24:23.350
It is really widely known among security people
because it's very useful if you want to bypass

01:24:23.350 --> 01:24:30.940
some filter, then you can perhaps use it,
but for developers, I mean, if they are interested

01:24:30.940 --> 01:24:36.440
in some low-level details of how JavaScript
works, then they know about JSPack, but many

01:24:36.440 --> 01:24:38.910
people didn't even hear about it.

01:24:38.910 --> 01:24:43.050
It was kind of surprising for me in the beginning
because it's like super interesting stuff

01:24:43.050 --> 01:24:52.040
about JavaScript, but the thing is that they
don't need to know that at all because why?

01:24:52.040 --> 01:24:56.940
They don't need to use these tricks, like,
they don't need to know that you can use,

01:24:56.940 --> 01:25:01.430
I don't know, array constructed constructor
to have access to a function that works like

01:25:01.430 --> 01:25:02.630
an even.

01:25:02.630 --> 01:25:06.199
It's super useful for security people, but
developers need that.

01:25:06.199 --> 01:25:14.340
Yeah, I've had a similar realization one day
when I think we were talking with the Java

01:25:14.340 --> 01:25:24.270
developer while developing Java deserialization
exploits or gadgets or whatever, or asking

01:25:24.270 --> 01:25:30.750
them about some reflections or whatever, and
they just didn't even know how to execute

01:25:30.750 --> 01:25:37.020
system-level code in Java because it is actually
a good thing because they don't do it in normal

01:25:37.020 --> 01:25:43.949
web applications, and we know a few ways in
every single language how to execute code.

01:25:43.949 --> 01:25:55.760
Yeah, so that was a really good insight that
the developers need other stuff than security

01:25:55.760 --> 01:26:02.650
people and something that might seem obvious
for us, even really obvious or even irrelevant

01:26:02.650 --> 01:26:03.650
for them.

01:26:03.650 --> 01:26:09.070
On the training, I also had some challenges
related to SQL injection, and you needed to

01:26:09.070 --> 01:26:17.699
bypass some filter, encode the data, escape
it somehow, and yeah, people didn't know many

01:26:17.699 --> 01:26:25.900
of the stuff because, let's be honest, if
you need to write a SQL query, then you are

01:26:25.900 --> 01:26:31.770
a developer, and you would try to write it
as legible as possible, and when we are trying

01:26:31.770 --> 01:26:35.400
to bypass some filters, then we are doing
pretty much the opposite.

01:26:35.400 --> 01:26:42.699
We are trying to use as many obscure facts
about how SQL is sparse as we possibly can,

01:26:42.699 --> 01:26:48.630
so yeah, at some point I understood that,
okay, I know that you probably, I mean I had

01:26:48.630 --> 01:26:56.320
some kind of cheat sheet from some point that
here are some useful features of SQL, JavaScript,

01:26:56.320 --> 01:27:04.630
whatever that you might need in the challenges,
but yeah, I remember that.

01:27:04.630 --> 01:27:07.140
In the very beginning, I was so surprised.

01:27:07.140 --> 01:27:08.830
How can people not know that?

01:27:08.830 --> 01:27:12.380
Yeah, okay, Michał, last question.

01:27:12.380 --> 01:27:17.780
I saw you started a YouTube channel yourself
recently.

01:27:17.780 --> 01:27:20.780
What are your plans regarding this?

01:27:20.780 --> 01:27:31.530
Yeah, so my plan, I don't have any specific
plans about what I want to do with the channel.

01:27:31.530 --> 01:27:33.270
Probably I will have some videos.

01:27:33.270 --> 01:27:41.270
I mean my first video was not technical, I
think not that technical, and it was pretty

01:27:41.270 --> 01:27:49.040
well received, which I think is pretty nice,
and I'll have some other ideas or other topics

01:27:49.040 --> 01:27:56.120
to talk about that are not purely technical,
but I think people might find them interesting.

01:27:56.120 --> 01:28:03.170
Like one of the topics is like the attack
scenarios and the threat model and like to

01:28:03.170 --> 01:28:07.430
understand what we can do with some kind of
attack.

01:28:07.430 --> 01:28:16.659
I specifically want to talk about HTML sanitization
on the client and why it makes sense, because

01:28:16.659 --> 01:28:27.300
recently I saw a YouTube short, I don't remember
what was the channel, but it was about the

01:28:27.300 --> 01:28:33.110
sanitizer API that is introduced in browsers,
and there were like many comments under that

01:28:33.110 --> 01:28:39.370
video about the sanitization must be done
on the server because you have it on the client,

01:28:39.370 --> 01:28:46.520
then it's bad, you can bypass it, you can
remove it, disable it, and yeah, that was

01:28:46.520 --> 01:28:47.900
an inspiration for me.

01:28:47.900 --> 01:28:55.460
Okay, I think that's something that we have
to explain to people so that they understand

01:28:55.460 --> 01:29:01.270
that it actually makes sense to sanitize on
the client, and that's a good excuse to have

01:29:01.270 --> 01:29:06.989
a little bit of a browser talk about threat
modeling, I think, in general.

01:29:06.989 --> 01:29:10.929
Yeah, we'll link this video in the description.

